\value{
\code{run_mvp()} is called for its side effect of launching the app and
returns the value of \code{\link[shiny:runApp]{shiny::runApp()}} invisibly once the app is closed.
\code{mvp_app()} returns a Shiny app object (class \code{shiny.appobj}).
}
\description{
\code{run_mvp()} launches the MVP app. \code{mvp_app()} returns the app as a Shiny app
object without running it, e.g. for deployment on Posit Connect or
shinyapps.io (an \code{app.R} containing \code{MVPapp::mvp_app()} is sufficient).

The app is evaluated in its own environment (whose parent gives access to
MVPapp and its key dependencies), so neither the global environment nor the
search path is modified. Global options changed by the app are set when it
starts and restored when it stops.
}
\details{
\strong{Objects kept for debugging.} MVPapp does not write to the global
environment. Objects useful for debugging are kept in an internal
environment, \code{MVPapp:::.mvp_state}, and remain available in the R session
after the app is closed. List them with \code{ls(MVPapp:::.mvp_state)}:
\itemize{
\item \code{last_ev_df$model_1}, \code{last_ev_df$model_2}: the dosing event tables used
in each model's most recent simulation (also used for the reproducible
bundle download).
\item \code{deps_env}: an internal cache used to run the app; not useful for debugging.
\item With \code{show_debugging_msg = TRUE}, AI translation responses: \code{llm_result}
(initial translation), \code{llm_refine} (latest retry that still failed),
\code{llm_success} (Dify's reply when told the code compiled), \code{branch_model} / \code{branch_refine} (raw
answers before cleaning) and \code{ellmer_chat_response} /
\code{dify_chat_response} (raw chat responses).
}

\strong{Branding.} For a company deployment, the title, colour, logo and favicon can be set when
launching, without editing any package files. Each is optional; with all of them left at
\code{NULL} the app looks as it always did.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{run_mvp(brand_color   = "#F36633",
        brand_logo    = "path/to/company_logo.png",
        brand_favicon = "path/to/favicon.png",
        page_title    = "Company Model Visualization Platform")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Image files are read from where they are and served by the app, so they do not need to be
inside the package (a web URL also works). A logo with a transparent background and a
height of about 30 pixels or more works best. \code{brand_color} replaces the navy in the
navbar, the active tab, the headers and borders of the panels, the data table headers and the
loading spinners. The same arguments work with \code{mvp_app()} for hosted deployments.

\strong{Hosting and security.} MVPapp evaluates R and C++ model code supplied
in the browser, so anyone who can open the app can run code on the server
under the server account. \code{authentication_code} and the model passwords
are a convenience to limit access to the interface and to hide models, not
a security boundary: host the app only for trusted users, in a sandboxed
account or container. The same applies to AI translation: its output is
compiled automatically, and compiling mrgsolve code can itself run
commands, so do not translate documents from untrusted sources on a
shared server.

Without installing MVPapp (e.g. where package installation is restricted),
the development version can also be launched from GitHub, provided its
dependencies are installed:
\code{shiny::runGitHub("MVPapp", "stevechoy", subdir = "inst/shiny")}.
}
